# Responses

A handler is a function from a context to a response. Everything else on
these pages is about getting input out of the request; this one is about
what you send back.

Howdy's responses are ordinary `Response` values from `gleam/http/response`.
The `controller` module has helpers for the common bodies, and because the
result is a plain value you can keep piping it, to change the status, add a
header or set a cookie.

## Text, HTML and JSON

There is a helper for each common kind of body. Each one answers `200` and
sets the matching `content-type`:

```gleam
import gleam/json
import howdy/controller.{type Context}

fn plain(ctx: Context) {
  controller.text(ctx, "Howdy!")
}

fn page(ctx: Context) {
  controller.html(ctx, "<h1>Howdy!</h1>")
}

fn data(ctx: Context) {
  controller.json(ctx, json.object([#("greeting", json.string("Howdy!"))]))
}
```

- `controller.text` sends `text/plain; charset=utf-8`.
- `controller.html` sends `text/html; charset=utf-8`.
- `controller.json` takes a `Json` value from `gleam/json`, turns it into a
  string for you, and sends `application/json; charset=utf-8`.

`controller.html` sends the string exactly as given. Build pages with a
library that escapes values for you, such as Lustre, and pass it the result
of `element.to_document_string`. Never join user input into HTML by hand.

If your handler gets its data from a function that can fail, look at
`service.respond` in [Services and errors](/docs/controllers/services). It
picks the status and body from a `Result`, so the handler does not have to.

## Status codes

`controller.with_status` changes the status of a response you have already
built. Use it when the body is right but `200` is not:

```gleam
fn create_user(ctx: Context) {
  controller.json(ctx, json.object([#("id", json.int(42))]))
  |> controller.with_status(201)
}
```

When there is no body to send, `controller.status` builds an empty response
with just a status:

```gleam
fn delete_user(ctx: Context) {
  controller.status(ctx, 204)
}
```

That is the usual answer to a successful `DELETE`, and a quick way to answer
`404` or `403` from a handler when a JSON error body is not wanted.

## Headers

Set headers with `response.set_header` from `gleam/http/response`. Header
names are lowercase. Setting one that a helper already set replaces it,
which is how you send a content type that has no helper of its own:

```gleam
import gleam/http/response

fn export_users(ctx: Context) {
  controller.text(ctx, "id,name\n1,Ada\n")
  |> response.set_header("content-type", "text/csv; charset=utf-8")
  |> response.set_header(
    "content-disposition",
    "attachment; filename=\"users.csv\"",
  )
}
```

The `content-disposition` header here asks the browser to download the body
as a file called `users.csv`, not to show it.

Headers that apply to every response, such as security headers, belong in
middleware, not repeated in each handler.

## Redirects

A redirect is an empty response with a `location` header. The status says
what kind of redirect it is:

```gleam
fn old_profile(ctx: Context) {
  controller.status(ctx, 301)
  |> response.set_header("location", "/account")
}
```

- `301` is permanent. Browsers and search engines remember it, so use it for
  pages that have moved for good.
- `302` is temporary: go here this time, but keep asking the original URL.
- `303` means "see other", and the browser always follows it with a `GET`.
  Use it after a form `POST`, so that refreshing the next page does not
  submit the form again. [Forms](/docs/controllers/forms) has an example.
- `307` and `308` are the temporary and permanent versions that keep the
  method and body, so a redirected `POST` stays a `POST`.

If any part of the location comes from the request, such as a `?next=`
parameter, check it before you redirect. Sending visitors to whatever URL a
link contains lets an attacker use your site's name to reach theirs. Only
accept paths that start with a single `/`.

## Cookies

`cookie.set` and `cookie.delete` are steps in the same pipeline. They add a
`set-cookie` header and leave everything else alone:

```gleam
import howdy/cookie

fn sign_out(ctx: Context) {
  controller.status(ctx, 303)
  |> response.set_header("location", "/")
  |> cookie.delete("session", cookie.defaults())
}
```

See [Cookies](/docs/controllers/cookies) for the options.

## Streaming a body

A body that takes a while to produce, such as a large export, can be sent
a piece at a time with `howdy/stream`, so the client receives each piece as
soon as it is ready and the whole body is never held in memory:

```gleam
import gleam/list
import gleam/result
import howdy/stream

fn export_users(_ctx: Context) {
  response.new(200)
  |> response.set_header("content-type", "text/csv; charset=utf-8")
  |> stream.body(fn(writer) {
    use writer <- result.try(stream.send_text(writer, "id,name\n"))
    list.try_fold(users.all(), writer, fn(writer, user) {
      stream.send_text(writer, user.id <> "," <> user.name <> "\n")
    })
  })
}
```

The function runs once the status and headers have been sent. Each
`stream.send` or `stream.send_text` hands back the writer for the next one,
and returning `Ok` ends the body. Returning `Error` stops writing and closes
the connection without ending the body, so the client can tell it was cut
short rather than mistaking half an export for all of it. The error is
logged, unless it came from a send that failed because the client had gone.

Because the status has been sent before the body starts, a stream cannot
change its mind and answer `500` part way through. Check what can be
checked first, and answer with an error before streaming if it fails.

`testing.text` and `testing.bytes` read a streamed body in full, so it is
tested like any other.

To push messages to a browser as things happen, rather than send one long
body, use [server-sent events](/docs/controllers/server-sent-events).

## Responses you do not write

Some responses never reach your handler, because Howdy sends them first:

- `404` when no route matches the path, and `405` with an `allow` header
  when the path matches but the method does not. Both have empty bodies.
- `204` with an `allow` header for an `OPTIONS` request to a path that has
  routes.
- A `HEAD` request runs the matching `GET` handler, and the server sends its
  status and headers without the body.

[Router](/docs/controllers/router) covers all of these. The input helpers in
`howdy/query`, `howdy/cookie`, `howdy/param` and `howdy/body` answer `400`
or `422` for you as well, always as JSON in the shape described in
[Services and errors](/docs/controllers/services).

## Testing responses

`howdy/testing` sends a request through your app and hands back the
response, which is the same plain value your handler built. Check the status
and headers directly, and read the body with `testing.text`:

```gleam
import gleam/http/response
import howdy/testing

pub fn export_is_a_download_test() {
  let res = testing.get("/users/export") |> testing.send(app())

  assert res.status == 200
  assert response.get_header(res, "content-type")
    == Ok("text/csv; charset=utf-8")
  assert testing.text(res) == "id,name\n1,Ada\n"
}
```

For a JSON body, `testing.json(res, decoder)` decodes it with a decoder from
`gleam/dynamic/decode`, so the test can assert on typed values, not on the
exact text of the JSON.
